course: dictionary
lesson: 15830
title: Underlying: the hidden part
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word underlying has two meanings. The first is real but hidden: it is the true reason behind something you can see. «The coach found the underlying cause of Mia's tiredness: she was not sleeping well.» The tiredness is what you see; the poor sleep is the hidden reason under it.
explain: The second meaning is about place: lying under something else. «The soft grass hid the rock underlying it.» The rock is under the grass, so underlying means lying beneath.
explain: Ask yourself: is something hidden below or behind what I can see? If yes, underlying probably fits. Swap in the words "hidden" or "lying under" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Underlying is an adjective, and it is also the -ing form of the verb underlie.
fact: «Underlying» means real but hidden, the true reason behind something.
fact: «Underlying» can also mean lying under something else, like rock under soil.
fact: In health, an underlying condition is an illness someone already has.
fact: Useful phrases: underlying cause, underlying problem, underlying condition.
exercise: «The pattern underlying the numbers is adding two each time.» What does underlying mean here? | The first number in the list | *The hidden rule behind the numbers | Something written in red ink | A number that is too big
exercise: «The soft grass hid the rock underlying it.» What does underlying mean in this sentence? | Shining brightly | *Lying under something else | Very old | Happy and bright
exercise: «Ben said he was fine, but his underlying worry was that he would fail the test.» What was the real reason Ben felt bad? | He was too tired to run | He was hungry | He lost his pencil | *A hidden worry about the test
test: «The doctor said the cough was a sign of an underlying illness.» What is an underlying illness? | A cough that is very loud | A holiday cold | *An illness someone already has, which shows up as other signs | A broken bone
test: The team fixed the problem, but not the real reason for it. Which phrase means the true reason behind a problem? | Loud noise | Bright lamp | Empty box | *Underlying cause
exam: «Maya's teacher looked past her smile to the underlying sadness.» What does underlying mean here? | Loud and cheerful | Very bright and shiny | Empty and cold | *Real but hidden, under the smile
